Idea: the circuit is already near-minimal at 3 ct-ct multiplications and 4 plaintext multiplications. I'm trimming ciphertext additions by reusing pq in A, using A = c1z − 2p − pq, and building the 4q and 5q terms with fewer steps. The gain will be small.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext c1z = z * c1;
  Ciphertext p = z * c3;
  Ciphertext q = z * c5;
  Ciphertext q2 = q + q;
  Ciphertext q4 = q2 + q2;
  Ciphertext pq = p - q4 - q;
  Ciphertext p2 = p + p;
  Ciphertext A = c1z - p2 - pq;
  Ciphertext inner4 = pq + u * q4;
  Ciphertext inner2 = inner4 + inner4;
  Ciphertext inner = inner2 + inner2;
  Ciphertext y = A + u * inner;
  y = y + c0;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: the circuit is at its floor in ct-ct and plaintext multiplications. I'm making a small change by taking the "×4" after the u·q product (and as one chain) instead of before, so more of the cheap additions happen after the multiplication. This gives a new circuit shape with the same multiplication counts.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext c1z = z * c1;
  Ciphertext p = z * c3;
  Ciphertext q = z * c5;
  Ciphertext uq = u * q;
  Ciphertext uq2 = uq + uq;
  Ciphertext uq4 = uq2 + uq2;
  Ciphertext q2 = q + q;
  Ciphertext q4 = q2 + q2;
  Ciphertext pq = p - q4 - q;
  Ciphertext p2 = p + p;
  Ciphertext A = c1z - p2 - pq;
  Ciphertext inner4 = pq + uq4;
  Ciphertext inner2 = inner4 + inner4;
  Ciphertext inner = inner2 + inner2;
  Ciphertext y = A + u * inner;
  y = y + c0;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: scale u by 4 once (u4 = 4u, two additions) instead of scaling several other terms. Since u·inner = u4·(pq + u4·q), the inner term needs no ×4 or ×16 doublings. It is still 3 ct-ct multiplications and 4 plaintext multiplications, with fewer ciphertext additions.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext u2 = u + u;
  Ciphertext u4 = u2 + u2;
  Ciphertext c1z = z * c1;
  Ciphertext p = z * c3;
  Ciphertext q = z * c5;
  Ciphertext q2 = q + q;
  Ciphertext q4 = q2 + q2;
  Ciphertext pq = p - q4 - q;
  Ciphertext p2 = p + p;
  Ciphertext A = c1z - p2 - pq;
  Ciphertext inner = pq + u4 * q;
  Ciphertext y = A + u4 * inner;
  y = y + c0;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: u only appears as u4 = 4u, and 4z² = (2z)². So I compute z2 = z + z and u4 = square(z2) directly, which drops the intermediate u and one addition. It is still 3 ct-ct multiplications, with the squaring operating on the same level.

